Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.680
Part of what's been fun about
collaborating on the mandalorian with

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:36.120
Lucasfilm and Disney is that we have
been able to see through a few technical

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.360
innovations and a few firsts that I
think you're going to have a lot of

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.460
impact on the way television and movies
are made moving forward in partnership

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:52.379
with ILM and epic we have put together a
system whereby which we can have game

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:57.810
engine real-time render and video wall
technology coming together to create a

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:01.220
backdrop for the big beautiful world of
Star Wars.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.840
The volume is 21 feet tall

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.320
it's 75 feet in diameter run by seven
machines

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.770
pumping the visuals onto the screen
that's that's being created in

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:15.480
pre-production and can be on the screen
within 24 hours of being final.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.220
It's incredibly impressive when you first
walk out there because it completely

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.420
surrounds your peripheral vision and you
really quickly forget that you're

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:29.130
indoors and you're not out on some
planet surface it feels like a real

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.350
three-dimensional environment
surrounding you because it is a

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:32.560
three-dimensional environment.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.659
You can allow your key creatives to all make

00:01:36.659 --> 00:01:42.330
decisions together so that the shots are
captured entirely in camera which allows

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.960
for a better performance.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.310
And what was so
exciting about this is by bringing those

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:52.680
people together things started to click
and we started to realize well let's not

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.729
just do green-screen interactive light
if we're gonna design the whole set and

00:01:56.729 --> 00:02:01.860
game engine ahead of time maybe we could
have some in-camera effects if you look

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.580
at visual effects heavy films you've got
a film set and then it's gonna go to

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.729
post and it's gonna get the world put in
here we're considering all of that at

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:14.280
the same time and how do we create a
background and foreground that live

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.760
together on the volume harmoniously.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:18.740
When we started to play with the idea of

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:22.680
using Unreal Engine for virtual
production that's one of the things that

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.579
Richard and John started to embrace is
that you've got this very dynamic world

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:31.050
where you can have randomization of
things and find the happy accident that

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:35.280
gives you the perfect shot being able to
see the actors point at things and see

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.459
what they're looking at was pretty
transformative it gave everybody context

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:41.430
with the added benefit
that if you want to move a mountain from

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:43.360
there to there you can do it instantly.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.739
You could switch between the Iceland

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.860
location to the desert location all
within the same day of shooting

00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:00.320
The ability to shoot a 10-hour dawn is
extraordinary to shoot any sequence

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:03.959
where you say oh this world's not quite
right let's just move it over a little

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:08.400
an extraordinary number of benefits
and advantages for shooting in that

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.360
environment it's mind-blowing what that
tool is.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:15.320
Shots of character in a vehicle

00:03:15.329 --> 00:03:19.019
traveling through a complex environment
it's always very difficult to do

00:03:19.019 --> 00:03:23.370
believably on stage LED screens are a
wonderful solution to that problem

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:28.440
because what you're doing is you're
taking this technique of image based

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.829
lighting that we've been using in
computer graphics for years but it use

00:03:31.829 --> 00:03:33.420
it to light a subject.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.599
And then we would do shoots
where we would texture map

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:42.150
real, lit surfaces on to our game engine
geo. And so the camera could move

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:46.920
anywhere we would do interiors like
Werner Herzog's office,

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:53.100
And then you started doing things like building
sets into it having half a spaceship with

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.269
reflective surfaces and so it became
exciting because by the end of the

00:03:57.269 --> 00:04:00.220
season it was like let's start designing
sets around what this could do well.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.520
Just like the good old days.

